WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.880
Birds of Prey is soaring to new heights.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:07.451
Just before its release this Friday, director
Cathy Yan's Birds of Prey (and the Fantabulous

00:00:07.451 --> 00:00:12.019
Emancipation of One Harley Quinn) is earning
rave reviews from critics, whose responses

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:16.740
have been officially recorded on Rotten Tomatoes
- and it's become clear that critics truly

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.480
enjoyed their time with Harley Quinn and her
gang of misfits.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.580
As of Thursday, February 6, Birds of Prey
holds an excellent 89 percent approval rating

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.160
on the review aggregator site.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:31.099
With Academy Award nominee Margot Robbie leading
Birds of Prey as Harley, the role she first

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:35.829
played in 2016's Suicide Squad, the film puts
the colorful criminal's narrative firmly in

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:39.920
her own hands, telling the story of Harley's
alliance with several other powerful women

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.019
to save a young girl named Cassandra Cain
from the wicked Black Mask and his psychotic

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.520
sidekick Victor Zsasz.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.880
Joining Harley to form the Birds of Prey gang
are Huntress, Renee Montoya, and Black Canary.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.420
As one of the most anticipated films of the
first half of 2020 and another entry into

00:00:55.429 --> 00:00:59.550
the female superhero canon, Birds of Prey
is seemingly set to follow in the footsteps

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:04.600
of critical successes like fellow DCEU flick
Wonder Woman and the MCU's Captain Marvel

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.660
– which is certainly encouraging as comic
book studios start to create more diverse

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.540
stories for all types of fans.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.100
And, according to Rotten Tomatoes, those fans
are over the moon.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.680
The general critical consensus on Birds of
Prey so far seems to be that above all else,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.520
the movie is just plain ol' fun.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.620
Over at Time Out, Helen O'Hara opened her
review by saying the, quote, "story [is] scrappy,

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.660
weird, and ultimately fun as hell."

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.000
She went on to conclude:

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.340
"Birds of Prey isn't interested in hitting
all the usual superhero movie beats.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.460
It works because we haven't seen this story
a thousand times before, and because it leaves

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.260
behind the grim-dark posturing of Suicide
Squad.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.760
It's nice to see a joker who doesn't take
herself too seriously."

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.920
Meanwhile, Culture Whisper's Ella Kemp wrote:

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.660
"Birds of Prey fizzes with madness, violence,
and blistering fun.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.780
Harley Quinn has always been an agent of chaos
- which is why her and the Joker were so well-suited

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.360
- and her standalone film lets this madness
run wild from the off."

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.000
Owen Gleiberman of Variety praised director
Cathy Yan for her keen eye and good taste

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:04.500
in music.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.200
He wrote:

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.140
"Directing her first studio feature, Cathy
Yan keeps it all hurtling along with impeccable

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.000
ferocity.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:14.840
Her action scenes have a deftly detonating
visual spaciousness, capped by crowd-pleasing

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.060
moments like the one where Harley, brandishing
a baseball bat, ricochets it off the ground

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.620
with perfect slow-mo timing.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.940
And Yan sprinkles just the right songs [...] through
what feels like a music-video comic-book jamboree,

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.960
one that effectively taps into timely undercurrents
of feminine rage."

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:30.300
"I love this chick.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.310
She's got rage issues—"

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:32.680
"I don't have rage issues!"

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:37.160
Writing for IndieWire, Kate Erbland argued
that while the film is perhaps a little too

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:42.340
ambitious, it works in the end, noting that
the film marks, quote, "a necessary reinvention

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:47.020
that adds dimension and depth to one of the
DC Expanded Universe's most distinctive characters."

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:47.920
She added:

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.800
"It may not be the promised total emancipation
(at least not yet), but it is fantabulous

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.800
in its own way."

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.780
Obviously, Birds of Prey enjoyed largely positive
reviews upon its release - but some critics

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.580
weren't completely crazy about Harley's wild
ride.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.920
John Defore of The Hollywood Reporter accused
the film of following all the rules it claims

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:06.540
to break.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:07.280
He wrote:

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.320
"Those hoping for a Deadpool-like fusion of
mayhem and wit should lower their expectations:

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:17.140
Harley may be known for her unpredictability,
but Birds plays by action-movie rules."

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:22.040
Over at Vulture, Allison Willmore said the
film provides, quote, "mixed results" as it,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.930
quote, "attempts to pry Harley away from the
dysfunctional relationship that has defined

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:29.920
her and let her fly free with her own sparkle-and
shattered-limb-filled adventure."

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.160
She continued:

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.280
"If the results are mixed, it's because the
movie devotes more thought to putting distance

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.680
between itself and Suicide Squad than to imagining
what an independent version of the character

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:40.860
is actually like."

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:45.600
Willmore wrapped up her review by describing
Harley Quinn as a, quote, "character too beloved

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.200
to be abandoned and too confounding to be
solved."

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.620
However, the harshest Birds of Prey review
thus far seems to be from The San Francisco

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.760
Chronicle's Mick LaSalle, who didn't mince
any words.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:56.760
His take?

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.360
"Birds of Prey [...] is more than horrible.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.620
It should not exist.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.500
Money should never have been raised for it.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.560
The screenplay should never have been filmed.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.420
Margot Robbie shouldn't have produced it.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.040
She certainly shouldn't have starred in it.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.760
It's just a terrible thing to inflict on audiences,
who, after all, didn't hurt anyone and just

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:13.980
hoped to have a nice time."

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.700
“Y'know, psychologically speaking, vengeance
rarely brings the catharsis we hope for."

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:20.440
"Yeah…"

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.939
All in all, it's encouraging that this female-led
film is packing a punch, especially after

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.380
this iteration of Harley Quinn made her debut
in the largely mediocre Suicide Squad.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.840
With most critics praising the film and just
a handful shooting it down, it seems like

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.380
Birds of Prey will fly high upon its release.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.439
But, as always, fans will need to form their
own opinions when the film opens in theaters

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.740
on Friday, February 7.

